# Files read by Linux tools inside a container build or a container: LF everywhere,
# including on a Windows checkout. A CRLF turns "#!/bin/sh" into "#!/bin/sh\r" and
# the container dies with "not found"; it also leaks "\r" into YAML values and
# Dockerfile instructions. (The app Dockerfile also strips \r from the two scripts
# it copies, as a second wall.)
#
# This block comes FIRST on purpose: a later pattern wins, so the *.ps1 rule below
# still gives deploy/package/*.ps1 the CRLF Windows PowerShell wants.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
deploy/** text eol=lf

# Windows shells REQUIRE CRLF in batch files. rag.bat sat in the tree with
# LF-only endings and cmd.exe mis-parsed it into garbage tokens
# ("'M' is not recognized as an internal or external command"), which reads as
# a broken install rather than a line-ending problem. Pin it so a checkout on
# any machine, and any tool that rewrites these files, produces CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
